# Inspector and Code Editor

## The host owns the inspector

Workflow inspectors belong to the host (ADR 0097, amended by 0157). `@catamorphic/ui` ships no Details or Code panel: `WorkflowEditor` passes `renderInspector` the selected node, `close`, the code, and read-only state, and hosts that compose their own chrome mount `WorkflowCanvas` inside a `WorkflowEditorScope`. No framework package depends on Monaco or imposes panel size or action chrome.

The desktop implementation is `apps/desktop/src/renderer/screens/workflow-screen.tsx` with its `workflow-*` components:

- The side panel opens only for a subject (a selected step, code, a run, automatic runs, or a change request) and closes with it. There is no panel toggle and no tab strip.
- Selecting a step shows its details; clearing the selection or pressing Escape closes them. While code is open, selection moves the editor instead of switching views. A change request follows the selection.
- Step details lead with a human label, description, behavior, and the information used (parameters merged with arguments, with provenance and friendly types). Raw expressions, policies, and trigger configuration go behind Technical details. Opening the source and asking the agent for a change stay easy to find.

## Bidirectional linking

All linking state lives in `useCodeEditorLink` (`packages/react/src/hooks/use-code-editor-link.ts`). Editors call it inside a `WorkflowEditorScope` and never read the selection atoms directly. The registry Monaco editor (`packages/registry/src/monaco-editor/`, copied into the desktop at `components/catamorphic/monaco-editor.tsx`) is the reference wiring; keep the two in step.

**Canvas to code.** When the selection changes from outside the editor, the hook emits `reveal: { range, key }`. `key` increases on every new selection so the same range re-reveals. A re-parse that keeps the same node id does not re-reveal. The editor then scrolls `range.startLine` into the center, selects the range with the cursor at its start, and focuses.

**Code to canvas.** On user cursor moves, the editor calls `handleCursorPositionChange({ line, column })`. `findNodeAtPosition` picks the smallest non-container node whose `sourceRange` contains the cursor, falling back to the smallest container (`CONTAINER_TYPES` in `find-node-at-position.ts`, including `input`, whose range is the whole workflow) on structural lines. Steps and `pause` leaves therefore win over their boundary.

**No feedback loops.** The editor sets `programmaticReveal` while applying a reveal so its own cursor move is ignored. The hook sets `cursorDrivenRef` when a cursor move selects a node so that selection does not reveal back into the editor.

## Monaco

TypeScript mode with highlighting, inline errors, hover types, completion, and line numbers. Diagnostic 2307 ("Cannot find module") is ignored because only the active workflow file is loaded into the model.
